this imposing structure holds a special  place in the hearts of many kashmiris  for centuries the grand mosque has been  a venue for prayers and an arena for  political activism but has largely  remained closed for nearly two and a  half years  i come every friday for prayers but all  in vain the mosque remains closed i feel  sad and distressed we believe our  prayers will be answered if we pray at  the jamia masjid on fridays it used to  give me solace  kashmir is claimed in full by india and  pakistan but governed by the two nations  in parts indian administered kashmir has  seen protests and violent uprisings  against new delhi's rule for decades the  grand mosque has played a role in  driving these  in august 2019 the government revoked  kashmir special status and imposed a  communications blackout it also shut  mosques in this muslim-dominated region  while others have reopened the grand  mosque's leader has been detained in his  home and the grounds have remained out  of bounds for friday prayers  in the past tens of thousands of muslims  attended these  there are some elements that want to  disturb places like these and create an  impression internationally that muslims  are being suppressed in india i think  security agencies took these precautions  to prevent that  in the heart of the main city srinagar  the mosque is a major meeting point for  people who live here  sermons would address kashmir's  political struggles while authorities  have closed it for extended periods in  the past its closure now has raised  concerns about religious freedom  in these times we are seeing  unprecedented suppression pressure and  tyranny across india particularly in  kashmir where even religious gatherings  are not allowed our grand mosque plays a  central role in its closure negates  india's claims of secularism and  democracy  the government has also blamed the  pandemic and says it will ask local  authorities for a security review to  reopen the mosque but kashmiris are  skeptical and say new delhi has made  similar excuses in the past to crack  down on their freedoms  al-jazeera new delhi
 

